rafted).\n- **TF-024B-STEP7-DEMO-001** (closed 2026-05-11): --restore real-repo round-trip evidence at `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/restore-roundtrip-evidence.md` — operator-interactive demo at dispatch-plan step 7 completed against `jrnb2024/scp-024b-extras-restore-test` throw-away repo. **Surfaced CRIT** (mock-masking: transform did not unwrap `*.enabled` sub-objects for `required_linear_history`/`allow_force_pushes`/etc → HTTP 422 on real PUT); closed in fix-round-22 with generic `{enabled: bool}` unwrap + PUT-body-shape assertion test. Round-trip succeeded post-fix; all 4 key fields matched state-A target. Throw-away repo cleanup deferred — current gh token lacks `delete_repo` scope.\n- **TF-024B-EXTRAS-2-RESTORE-PATH-CONTAINMENT-001** (open): apply `resolve_inside_repo_root()` guard to `scripts/enable-required-check.sh --restore <path>` argument in 024B-extras-2 alongside other defensive hardening. Low blast-radius (operator-credentials-bound). Owner: 024B-extras-2 slice; does NOT gate 024C.\n- **TF-024B-EXTRAS-2-COHORT-REGEX-MULTICHAR-001** (open): widen `.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml` cohort regex from `024[C-F]/` to `024[C-F][^/]*/` for future multi-char cohort slice IDs. Current 5-cohort set (024C/D/E/F) is single-char and unaffected; defensive. Owner: 024B-extras-2 slice; does NOT gate 024C.\n- **TF-024B-STEP7-FIXTURE-SEQ-001** (open): align DISPATCH-NOTE step-7 fixture sequence with `restore-roundtrip-evidence-PLACEHOLDER.md` (add or harmonise `Mutate branch protection` step description). Will be resolved naturally at step-7 demo execution time.\n- **TF-024B-EXTRAS-2-STAGED-RESTORE-TEST-001** (open): add test case for staged-but-uncommitted prior-restore evidence in 024B-extras-2 tests (guards against future narrowing of `git diff HEAD` to `git diff`). Owner: 024B-extras-2 slice.\n- **TF-024B-EXTRAS-2-SET-EQ-001** (open): restore verify-phase set-equality comparison (deferred from 024B-extras-1 carve per `SCOPE-CORRECTION-2-2026-05-11.md`). Closure: `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` verify phase uses jq sorted-set comparison for `required_status_checks.contexts`. NOT BLOCKING 024C — extras-1's substring grep -F is sufficient for cohort onboarding under invariant 7's <30-min SLO. Owns: 024B-extras-2 slice. Does NOT gate 024C.\n- **TF-024B-EXTRAS-2-RESTORE-FILE-TOCTOU-001** (open): `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` reads RESTORE_PRE_STATE file three separate times (cat to RESTORE_TARGET_JSON, validate_restore_source_json, inline Python signatures extract). Mitigation: read once into a variable and pipe content to validate + signature-extract via stdin. Defensive hardening; current posture-degradation flag gates bound the blast radius. Owner: 024B-extras-2 slice; does NOT gate 024C.\n- **TF-024B-EXTRAS-2-WORKFLOW-PIN-BASE-BRANCH-001** (open): `.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml` invokes `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` from the PR's checked-out code (PR HEAD), not the base branch. For future multi-operator adoption, pin enforcement to `git show origin/main:scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh | bash -s -- ...` or use a reusable workflow from base. Owner: 024B-extras-2 slice (depth-defense); does NOT gate 024C.\n- **TF-024B-REQCHECK-ENABLE-001** (open): Before 024C dispatch opens, operator must add `check-invocation-log-entry / check-invocation-log-entry` to main branch's `required_status_checks` list via `gh api -X PATCH repos/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/branches/main/protection` with the updated contexts list, then record the SCP-self REQCHECK enablement as a `STATUS.md` chain entry only. Do NOT modify `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`; that log is reserved for adopter-side `enable-required-check.sh` invocations per D-035 scope. Without this step CI-enforcement is advisory only (the workflow runs but doesn't block merge). Close as part of 024C kickoff ceremony.\n- **TF-024B-SCAFFOLDER-023E002-UPDATE** (open): when TF-023E-002 closes, remove the post-v1.2.0 warning block from `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` and delete the cutover ancestry guard that keeps the v1.0.0 fallback recommendation alive. The scaffolder should stop warning only after the separate `attest-scorecard` workflow restructuring lands. Owner: @jrnb2024.\n\n=====\n\nIf the wrapper content cannot be read from the current context, the helper requires `--i-understand-wrapper-inaccessible` before it will continue without wrapper-pin verification.\n\nIf the wrapper has had no successful runs in the past 60 days due to extended pre-Gate-2 breakage, append `--i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci` to bypass the cold-start safety check (the flag's name is from the cold-start case; semantically it gates the same condition).\n\n> **Post-break-glass permanence:** After any break-glass cycle, all subsequent forward-mode `enable-required-check.sh` invocations for the same adopter permanently require `--expected-wrapper-sha` (or `--i-understand-no-gate-2-verification` with CAUTION audit). This is intentional: once an adopter has hit a break-glass, we demand operator-explicit SHA confirmation on every re-enable for the rest of the adopter's lifetime. The restore log entry in git history is the authoritative record; force-push to main is disabled (D-030) so the entry cannot be expunged.\n\n> **WARNING.** `--i-understand-no-gate-2-verification` is an emergency-only bypass for Gate 2. It re-arms the gate against the current wrapper SHA, so if the wrapper is still pinned to a defective SCP SHA the gate will block adopters again.\n\n```bash\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh \\\n    --repo <owner/repo> \\\n    --branch <branch> \\\n    --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification\n```\n\n> **CAUTION.** When the bypass flag is used, the helper emits a caution line in the invocation log block, prints a CAUTION line to standard error, and pauses 5 seconds before any API mutation so the operator can abort with Ctrl-C if the flag was passed unintentionally. Use it only when the operator is intentionally prioritising recovery over wrapper-pin verification.\n\n> **🛑 CAUTION:** Re-enabling required-check while the adopter wrapper is pinned to a broken/unfixed SHA will block ALL future PR merges on the adopter repo until manually unpinned. There is no scriptable rollback for this misuse - the operator must run `--restore` again with the pre-Gate-1 captured pre-state JSON.\n\nReference: D-047, WP-SCP-024 invariant 7, D-035, D-030, and the break-glass guidance in `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/DISPATCH-NOTE.md`.\n\n## 13. Recommended Adoption Phases\n\n### Phase 0 — Prep\n\n- choose owners\n- choose pilot subsystem\n- add overlay directory\n- add wrapper scripts\n- update governance docs\n\n### Phase 1 — Local advisory use\n\n- run `consult` before implementation work\n- run `audit-changed` locally before PRs\n\n=====\n| D-036 | 2026-05-01 | Adopt `policies/VERSIONING.md` MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH semver contract on the SCP federation primitive's public surfaces (`workflow_call.inputs` on `policy-check.yml`, `schemas/policy-check-summary.schema.json` + `schemas/rule-config.schema.json`, `SCP-R-NNN` rule IDs) WITH a one-release deprecation ramp before any breaking change AND `docs/reviews/rule-proposals/` RFC-lite process (quorum=1 SCP-CODEOWNER per D-031 single-operator mode; 48h wall-clock window with weekend-counts; 0 approvals = auto-defer with `defer` label; bypass-introducing proposals require explicit §5 enumeration and a non-waivable 48h window per 020H.1 R1 SAFE-MAJ-001 closure) as estate doctrine for SCP federation primitive versioning and rule governance. Closes WP-SCP-020 §4 020H.1 (i)+(ii)+(iii) and BS-5. Forward-compat to 2-of-N quorum when a second maintainer onboards (D-031 escalation path, 2026-07-21 review). Internal-only surfaces (`lib/policy_check_invocation.sh`, `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock`, helper scripts in `scripts/`, Renovate preset internal shape, OPA Rego helpers in `policies/scp_common.rego`) are explicitly NOT covered — they may be refactored without notice | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-035 (020G adopter-helper invocation): every new governance procedure binding adopter behaviour ships a ratifying D-NNN row to make the doctrine searchable in the canonical decision-record home rather than buried in a slice DISPATCH-NOTE. The one-release deprecation ramp is the MINIMUM version-skew adopters must tolerate before a surface vanishes; combined with the Renovate preset's automated SHA-pin bumps, this gives every adopter at least one full PR cycle's worth of warning annotations before a breaking change lands. The RFC-lite process is intentionally lightweight at v1.0.0 because the SCP estate is single-operator; the auto-defer mechanism is currently a manual close + label step. As estate scale grows (WP-SCP-024 cascade), the auto-defer mechanism may be lifted to a GitHub Action that runs on a schedule. Identified at WP-SCP-020 §4 020H.1 plan row + 020H.1 R1 completeness COMP-MAJ-003 closure |\n| D-037 | 2026-05-01 | Grant `issues: write` permission to the `release-gate-violation-issue` job in `.github/workflows/release-gate.yml` (per WP-SCP-022 020H.3.1, closing TF-020H3rg-003). Privilege scope is the JOB level (not workflow default); the release-gate evaluation job continues to run with `contents: read` only. The new job fires only on `needs.release-gate.result == 'failure' && github.event_name == 'push'` — workflow_dispatch dry-runs and clean push:tags runs never reach it. The job's only mutation is `gh issue create` / `gh issue comment` / `gh label create --force` against the SCP source repo's own Issues namespace; no other write surface is unlocked. The auto-opened issue assigns `@jrnb2024` (single-operator mode per D-031); forward-compat env `SCP_RELEASE_GATE_ASSIGNEES` for 2nd-maintainer escalation. Symmetric with D-029 (020C.1 `statuses: write` for read-back commit-status) — a privilege-write expansion ratified by an amending decision row that names the precise threat-surface and bounded behaviour | ACCEPTED | The release-gate's last-line-of-defense alarm needs SOME write surface to surface beyond the workflow log; opening a GitHub issue is the lightest-weight signal that survives operator absence (the workflow log is ephemeral; the issue persists + assigns + sends a notification). Privilege-of-least-privilege is honoured by job-level scoping rather than workflow-level — the gate-evaluation steps (pip install, schema validate, ramp check, expired-config check) cannot accidentally open issues because they run in the release-gate job which has only `contents: read`. CODEOWNERS coverage on `.github/**` (per WP-SCP-020 020K) gates any change to release-gate.yml itself. The forward-compat env mirrors `SCP_REGRESSION_ASSIGNEES` from canary-replay.yml. Identified at WP-SCP-022 020H.3.1 R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 + SAFE-MAJ-001 closures |\n| D-038 | 2026-05-02 | Adopt hash-pinned Python supply-chain installs across the SCP federation primitive's two YAML/JSON-validating workflows (`.github/workflows/policy-check.yml` × 4 install sites, `.github/workflows/release-gate.yml` × 1 install site) via `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements/policy-check.txt`. Top-level pins (`pyyaml==6.0.2` + `jsonschema==4.23.0`) match the prior inline pins exactly — no version bump on the public Python deps; only the install mechanism is hardened. Lockfile generated by `pip-compile --generate-hashes` from `requirements/policy-check.in`; full transitive closure (`attrs`, `jsonschema-specifications`, `referencing`, `rpds-py`) hash-pinned with all-platform wheel SHA256s. Closes WP-SCP-022 TF-020H3rg-002 (filed at 020H.3 R1 safety MIN-SAFE-006 — a PyPI compromise of either pinned version could substitute a malicious package on the next install; `--require-hashes` causes pip to refuse install when the served wheel's SHA256 is not in the lockfile). PATCH bump (v1.0.0 → v1.0.1) per `policies/VERSIONING.md` PATCH category (\"internal refactors of helpers/lib\"); no `policies/deprecations.yaml` entry required (no surface deprecation, no behaviour change for adopters). Lockfile regeneration command captured in `requirements/policy-check.txt` header AND `requirements/policy-check.in` documentation block | ACCEPTED | Symmetric supply-chain hardening with the pre-existing `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock` (OPA + Conftest + Regal binary SHA256 verification) and Sigstore attestation soft-warn for OPA (TF-007). All three layers — Python deps, OPA-family binaries, GitHub Actions SHA pins — are now hash-anchored at SCP source. The lockfile + `.in` source pair mirrors `scripts/.tool-versions` + `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock` (input + lockfile pattern). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged for lockfile regeneration: an operator who regenerates without re-reviewing the pip-compile output could introduce drifted transitive versions; mitigation is the `requirements/policy-check.in` source-of-truth (pins only the top-level deps; transitive resolution is automatic but reviewable in the diff). Future upgrades to `pyyaml` / `jsonschema` itself follow the same path: edit `.in`, regenerate `.txt`, review diff. The Sigstore re-tightening (TF-007) is parallel work on a different supply-chain dimension. Identified at 020H.3 R1 safety MIN-SAFE-006 closure |\n| D-039 | 2026-05-02 | Extend the slice 020M hash-pinned Python supply-chain install pattern to the SCP-self conflict-gate workflow (`.github/workflows/conflict-gate.yml` \"Install Python dependencies\" step) per WP-SCP-022 slice 020N, closing TF-020M-001. New `requirements/conflict-gate.in` (top-level pins matching `pyproject.toml` `[project.dependencies]` + `[project.optional-dependencies.dev]`: fastapi, uvicorn, httpx, jsonschema, pydantic, pyyaml, pytest, ruff, mypy) + generated `requirements/conflict-gate.txt` (pip-compile --generate-hashes under Python 3.12 to match `setup-python: python-version: \"3.12\"`; full transitive closure with all-platform wheel SHA256s). Install pattern: (i) `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements/conflict-gate.txt` (PyPI deps); (ii) `pip install --no-deps vendor/python/ct_auth-0.8.0-py3-none-any.whl` (local vendored wheel — not on PyPI; CODEOWNERS-protected via `vendor/** @jrnb2024`); (iii) `pip install --no-deps -e .` (editable local SCP package without re-resolving deps already hash-verified in step (i)); (iv) post-install version-pin assertion (`assert yaml.__version__ == \"6.0.3\"; assert jsonschema.__version__ == \"4.26.0\"; assert fastapi.__version__ == \"0.136.1\"; assert pydantic.VERSION == \"2.13.3\"`) defending against future-PR drift (mirrors 020M SAFE-MAJ-002 pattern). Deliberate pyyaml-version drift accepted: conflict-gate.txt resolves pyyaml==6.0.3, policy-check.txt pins pyyaml==6.0.2 — independent workflows with independent supply-chain anchors; both hash-anchored. **No semver bump.** Per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\", `conflict-gate.yml` is not in the public-surface enumeration (workflow_call inputs on `policy-check.yml`, schema contracts, rule IDs); it is a SCP-self-only workflow that adopters do not consume. Therefore no `policies/deprecations.yaml` entry, no version-manifest bump, no release notes — internal hardening | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-038 (slice 020M hash-pinning of policy-check.yml + release-gate.yml). The conflict-gate is the third Python-installing workflow in the SCP repo; closing the unhardened install completes the Python supply-chain hardening across all three SCP-self workflows that pip-install at runtime. Threat surface (PyPI compromise of conflict-gate's deps) was acknowledged at 020M R1 safety as MIN-class because conflict-gate is SCP-self-only (not adopter-facing); a compromise would degrade SCP self's policy-evaluator quality but not directly compromise adopters. The user's directive to clear all outstanding TFs (rather than defer to v1.1 maintenance) raised this from forward-compat to in-flight closure. The `--no-deps` editable install of the local SCP package is the tradeoff: pip refuses `--require-hashes` on editable installs, so the local source tree is installed without re-resolving (its PyPI deps are already hash-verified above). The `ct-auth` vendored wheel is similarly outside `--require-hashes` (pip refuses for local file: URLs); chain-of-trust is the `vendor/**` CODEOWNERS coverage + the wheel being in-tree (no network fetch). Identified at 020M R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 closure |\n| D-040 | 2026-05-02 | Amend the rule-RFC process (`docs/reviews/rule-proposals/README.md` §Process step 2) so that the 48h wall-clock review window is a CEILING (auto-defer trigger) rather than a FLOOR (minimum wait time) **in single-operator mode** (D-031 default at v1.0.0). Rationale: in single-operator mode the proposal author IS the only `SCP-CODEOWNERS` member; there are no external reviewers for the wall-clock pause to enable. The recursive 3-lens R1/R2 adversarial review (per `feedback_recursive_adversarial_review.md`) IS the adversarial-review surface; the wall-clock pause was vestigial theatre that produced no additional safety. The operator MAY merge a proposal as soon as adversarial review reaches fixpoint (no new CRIT/MAJ on a complete cycle) AND the proposal PR is open with the `scp-rule-proposal` label applied. The 48h CEILING remains in force: zero approvals by 48h still triggers auto-defer (`defer` label per slice 020L label-provisioning). When a second maintainer onboards (D-031 escalation, 2026-07-21 review), the 48h becomes a mandatory MINIMUM to give the second maintainer time to review — the CEILING-not-FLOOR posture is single-operator-only. Bypass-introducing proposals (`Bypass-surface non-empty: true`) retain their non-waivable 48h MINIMUM regardless of operator-count posture, because the additional adversarial-review value of a wall-clock pause IS material when a new bypass surface is being introduced (the operator may catch their own bypass-introducing PR's flaws on second look more reliably than for a non-bypass additive rule). Surfaced by 020L dogfood (the first RFC actually walked through the process). Closes WP-SCP-022 020L post-R2 process-gap | ACCEPTED | The 020L dogfood surfaced this gap operationally: the operator (single-maintainer) reached R1+R2 fixpoint on a non-bypass proposal and was about to wait 48h before merging — at which point the wait was identified as theatre rather than safety (no second pair of eyes to chime in). Theatre-vs-safety is the doctrine question the dogfood is designed to surface cheaply. The amendment narrows the discretionary posture to single-operator + non-bypass + post-fixpoint, leaving every other shape of the 48h window unchanged: bypass-introducing proposals keep the non-waivable minimum (the threat-surface justifies the wait); the post-2nd-maintainer-onboard 48h becomes mandatory minimum so the second maintainer has time to review; the 48h CEILING for auto-defer remains so abandoned proposals are surfaced. Symmetric with D-033's \"operational reality must amend the spec when the spec encounters a constraint that wasn't anticipated\" pattern. Identified at WP-SCP-022 slice 020L post-R2 fixpoint by operator (`@jrnb2024`) when reviewing the merge-readiness of PR #89 |\n| D-041 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard data shape (`schemas/scorecard-emit.schema.json` v0.1) + opt-in adopter participation model per WP-SCP-023 023B (this PR). The schema defines aggregated per-rule counts (raw_findings, denies, waived, rule_config_disabled), waivers aggregate (active_count, by_rule_id, expiring_within_30d), rule-config aggregate (disabled_rules, expiring_within_30d), repo / ref / commit / scp_version / RFC3339 emitted_at, and a verdict (allow/deny/warn). The schema EXPLICITLY DOES NOT include waiver content (`reason`, `approved_by`, `waiver_id` strings) — invariant 2 of WP-SCP-023 plan-doc binds this. The reusable workflow gains a `scorecard-emit: bool` `workflow_call` input (default false) so existing adopters see no behaviour change; opt-in adopters set `with: scorecard-emit: true`. Schema versioning: MAJOR-pinned per `policies/VERSIONING.md` D-036 + WP-SCP-023 plan-doc §10 Q1 — aggregator drops emits with unknown `schema_version`. Verification: GitHub Actions Artifact Attestation (OIDC-based) via `actions/attest-build-provenance@v4.1.0` (SHA `a2bbfa25...`) at emit time; aggregator verifies via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow` (per D-042 to be filed at slice 023C). v1.2.0 cut on this slice's merge per VERSIONING.md MINOR category | ACCEPTED | This is the first slice of WP-SCP-023's implementation chain (023A plan-doc landed at PR #97, commit `7075234`, 2026-05-03 R4 fixpoint). The data shape was specified at plan-doc level; this slice ratifies it as code surface. Default-false on the workflow input is the load-bearing safety property: existing adopters cannot have new behaviour spring on them; opt-in is explicit per invariant 4. The waiver-content exclusion (invariant 2) is enforced by the schema's `additionalProperties: false` constraint on every level + the explicit field enumeration. The MAJOR-pinning posture means schema breakage requires v2.0.0 + adopter migration; v0.1 prefix on `schema_version` reflects the freedom to amend until first adopter opts in. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: aggregator runs on the SCP repo with single-operator identity (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-041 reservation |\n| D-042 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard aggregator pipeline trust model per WP-SCP-023 023C (this PR). Mechanism: per-emit verification via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow <expected_workflow_ref>` (the GitHub Actions Artifact Attestation OIDC pattern, mandatory per the D-042 reservation note above + 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001 closure); index trust rooted in git's required-signed-commits (slice 020J) + `output/scorecards/** @jrnb2024` CODEOWNERS coverage (slice 023A) + branch protection on main (slice 020D2), NOT a separate Ed25519 key layer. The aggregator runs as `.github/workflows/scorecard-aggregator.yml` on a weekly cron (Monday 06:00 UTC) + `workflow_dispatch`; two-job structure (aggregate read-only + commit write-scoped via job-level permissions, mirroring the 023B `attest-scorecard` job split); reads `docs/scorecards/opt-in-registry.yaml` (CODEOWNERS-protected at `docs/scorecards/**`), pulls each adopter's latest green policy-check run's `scorecard-emit.json` artifact, verifies attestation, schema-validates emit, aggregates into `output/scorecards/index.json` (schema: `schemas/scorecard-index.schema.json`), and opens a PR for operator review. Failure modes (verification_failure / unreachable / no_emit) record loudly per-adopter — never silently dropped. NEVER carries waiver content (invariant 2 enforced by schema `additionalProperties: false` at every level). Internal-only workflow per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\" (mirrors 020N conflict-gate.yml posture); no semver bump. Verifies WP-SCP-023 plan-doc invariant 6 (\"Aggregator output is Ed25519-signed\") in spirit — Sigstore/Fulcio uses Ed25519 under the hood, managed by GitHub rather than by an SCP-side private key, eliminating the private-key-distribution surface | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-041 (per-emit data shape + opt-in model) — D-041 ratified the EMITTER side of the trust model; D-042 ratifies the AGGREGATOR / VERIFIER side. The mandatory `--signer-workflow` constraint was already pre-committed at the D-042 reservation note above (closes 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001) — this slice ratifies it as workflow code. The Ed25519-via-Sigstore framing eliminates the chicken-and-egg of \"where does the SCP private key live and how does the aggregator hold it without exposing adopters\" — GitHub manages the keys via OIDC + Sigstore. The two-job permission split (aggregate read-only + commit write-scoped) is identical to 023B's `policy-check` + `attest-scorecard` split (closes the analogous threat surface where workflow-level write permissions could be exploited by a compromised aggregate-side step). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: aggregator runs on the SCP repo with single-operator identity (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-042 reservation + 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001 closure |\n| D-043 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard exposure surface — markdown report (`docs/scorecards/<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`) + MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard` — per WP-SCP-023 023D (this PR). Markdown generator at `scripts/generate-scorecard-report.py` produces deterministic golden-file-tested output reading `output/scorecards/index.json`; aggregator workflow `.github/workflows/scorecard-aggregator.yml` extended in the commit job to invoke the generator + add the rendered markdown to the operator-review PR alongside the index. MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard` (registered in `src/standards_control_plane/mcp_server/tools.py`): read-only contract per WP-SCP-023 plan-doc invariant 5; request shape `{repo_filter?, since_emitted_at?}`; response shape excludes waiver content (`reason` / `approved_by` / `waiver_id` — invariant 2) by Pydantic model construction (extra=forbid + explicit field whitelist). The MCP method reads `output/scorecards/index.json` from the SCP repo working copy at call time; staleness is observable via the response's `aggregated_at` field. ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 documents the adopter onboarding workflow. NEVER carries waiver content (invariant 2 enforced by Pydantic models + schema + dedicated pytest scans on both markdown + MCP responses) | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-041 + D-042 — D-043 ratifies the CONSUMER side: markdown for operators/auditors, MCP for agents/tooling. Read-only contract + Pydantic-enforced field whitelist + golden-file pytest together preserve invariant 2 across both surfaces. The MCP method does NOT introduce a new authentication primitive — it rides on WP-SCP-021's existing Ed25519 receipt mechanism (consult-class semantics). The markdown report is informational and CODEOWNERS-protected via `docs/scorecards/** @jrnb2024`; operators can hand-edit before merging the aggregator PR if needed (regenerator overwrites on next run). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: the MCP server runs at acc.brokapps.ai; if down, agents lose `consult_scorecard` access but the markdown report + git index remain consultable. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-043 reservation |\n| D-044 | 2026-05-09 | Adopt the WP-SCP-024 slice 024B-core operational contracts for cascade scaffolding. Components: (1) `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` (`SCP-073-scaffolder` per WP-SCP-020 §11 follow-up + WP-SCP-021 follow-up `SCP-075-scaffolder-compose`) generates adopter-side artefacts mechanically - bootstrap-only (refuses `CI=true` / `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true` per D-035 symmetry); validates `--scp-sha` against SCP main HEAD lineage (warns if not main HEAD; refuses if non-existent); emits a TF-023E-002 warning for post-v1.2.0 SHAs while recommending the v1.0.0 pin; `--scp-sha` 40-char hex regex; `--default-branch` enum {main, master, develop}; `--scorecard-emit` enum; `--output-dir` writability + path-normalised system-path denylist (incl `/private/var/*` macOS canonicalisation); emits `MANIFEST.json` with SHA256 audit; arguments per plan-doc §5.3. Canonical adopter slugs are normalization aids only, not collision-resistant IDs, so cohort repo names must remain unique after canonicalization. (2) `templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl` is the canonical adopter wrapper shape per ADOPT-001 §12 with Renovate inline-comment marker `# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane` enabling auto-bump per plan-doc invariant 8 Threshold A criterion; substitutions `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, `{{SCP_SHA}}`, `{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`; least-privilege workflow-level permissions (`contents:read` + `statuses:write` only). (3) `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` CLI tool implements plan-doc invariant 2 cascade-status CI enforcement: 4 enforcement behaviours (fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised; `onboarded` -> log-entry-modified + target-match; `onboarded-operator-bump` -> log entry + `STATUS.md` `TF-024X-renovate-<slug>` row matching invariant 2 regex literally; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` -> `DISPATCH-NOTE` `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference + log file NOT modified) + explicit tooling-slice opt-in gate for `cascade-status: not applicable` (`--allow-not-applicable`; default refusal for direct CLI use). Regex via `python3 re.search()` (PCRE-style); exactly-one-match assertions on both `cascade-status` and `**Target:**` extraction close multi-occurrence injection bypasses; adopter-slug canonicalisation per plan-doc spec. CLI tool only - GitHub Actions workflow wiring (`.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml`) defers to slice 024B-extras alongside `enable-required-check.sh --restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass procedure. D-044 was reserved for slice 024B at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge (PR #102, 2026-05-04); after the operator-decided scope-split (SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md), D-044 stays scoped to core deliverables; sibling D-NNN may be reserved at 024B-extras authoring time for the deferred surface (`--restore` + break-glass + workflow wiring). Slice 024B-core ships under documented protocol deviation per feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md note + justify rule (kernel hook lives in ACC repo only; per-repo install pattern is FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 - open). | ACCEPTED | D-044 was reserved at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge per the §9 reservation block. The original 024B accumulated 7 fix-rounds + 7 R-rounds against a single branch with each round closing 5-6 MAJ but introducing 2-3 new defects in just-added code (R6 missed primary-deliverable workflow wiring; R7 fix introduced bootstrap defect on slice's own PR). Empirical signal: surface area too large (16 files, ~3500 lines added) for single-pass convergence. Operator decided 2026-05-09 to split 024B into 024B-core (this slice; ~5 files; scaffolder + template + CI enforcement script + tests) + 024B-extras (deferred; `--restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass + `.github/workflows/` CI wiring + step 7 operator-interactive demo). Split is incremental delivery, NOT descope - both halves still ship; both required before 024C cohort onboarding opens. Original 024B work preserved at `feature/wp-scp-024-024b-extras-parking` branch (audit trail: 7 codex-fix-round-N-audit.log files + 7 R-round archive directories incl r8-archive carry-forward findings). Slice 024B-core scope is internally coherent: scaffolder generates adopter artefacts; CI enforcement script (CLI) validates cascade-status declarations; tests cover both. The CLI script can be invoked manually as a stop-gap until 024B-extras wires it into GitHub Actions. The CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient: workflow-side hardcoding of `--tooling-slice-id` and omission of `--allow-not-applicable` in 024B-extras is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation |\n| D-047 | 2026-05-10 | Adopt WP-SCP-024 024B-extras operational contracts: `scripts/enable-required-check.sh --restore <pre-state.json>` restores branch protection from the captured prior-state log entry by transforming GET-shape JSON into the PUT body, stripping envelope fields recursively, converting `enforce_admins.enabled` to a boolean, and replaying `required_signatures` through the dedicated sub-resource; restore-mode posture-degradation flags `--i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement`, `--i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks`, `--i-understand-restore-disables-strict-mode`, `--i-understand-restore-disables-required-signatures`, `--i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes`, and `--i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions` are mandatory when the target removes or re-enables those protections; forward-mode now performs a path-pinned workflow lookup for `.github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml` and queries workflow-runs without a branch filter; the new CI workflow `.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml` wires the CLI gate into PR checks with `paths: docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[C-Z]*/**` plus explicit branch-protection-log.md / STATUS.md triggers and tooling-slice short-circuiting; ADOPT-001 gains §12.8 break-glass procedure with manual Gate 3 verification; STATUS.md and the DISPATCH-NOTE placeholder for the step-7 operator demo are updated in-slice. The depth-defense surface (wrapper-SHA verification, canonical-context guard, set-equality verify) is carved into sibling slice 024B-extras-2 per `SCOPE-CORRECTION-2-2026-05-11.md`; D-048 reserved for that slice. | ACCEPTED | The 024B split left the restore / break-glass / workflow-integration surface as the deferred half so the review load stayed bounded. This decision ratifies the operational contract that lets operators disable and re-enable the federation primitive safely without widening the adopter-facing API surface, while preserving the bootstrap guard that prevents tooling slices from self-blocking their own PRs. The release-tag SHA requirement is intentionally anchored to actual git tags, not arbitrary commits, so Gate 3 can re-arm against a concrete release artifact rather than against moving main-head. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: the helper remains operator-run, the escape hatch is explicitly auditable, and the restore demo evidence is still an operator-interactive step outside Codex dispatch. Gate 2 automated wrapper-SHA verification is absent in extras-1; the trust-model gap is deferred to D-048 / 024B-extras-2, which will add an `--i-understand-no-gate-2-verification` flag for explicit operator acknowledgement when Gate 2 verification is skipped. Identified at WP-SCP-024 024B-extras authoring time. |\n| D-048 | 2026-05-13 | Adopt the WP-SCP-024 024B-extras-2 depth-defense surface as estate doctrine. Components: (1) `--expected-wrapper-sha SHA` flag + `validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags()` validates the supplied SHA against `gh api repos/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/git/refs/tags?per_page=100` with annotated-tag dereferencing (per-annotated-tag `gh api git/tags/<sha>` follow-up to resolve to commit SHA), so Gate 3 SHA-pinning works regardless of whether SCP-self uses lightweight or annotated release tags. (2) `--i-understand-no-gate-2-verification` flag is the audited Gate 2 bypass: emits a CAUTION line to stderr, a `GATE2_CAUTION_LINE` row in the invocation-log block, and pauses 5 seconds before any API mutation so operators can Ctrl-C if the flag was passed unintentionally; the bypass is only allowed when `prior_restore_evidence_present` returns true (Gate 3 re-enable post-break-glass), so cold-start adopters cannot accidentally bypass Gate 2. (3) `--i-understand-wrapper-inaccessible` flag is required when the adopter wrapper file (`.github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml`) cannot be read from the current context — without the flag, the script refuses to continue rather than silently degrading to a CAUTION-only audit note. (4) `--i-understand-restore-replaces-required-check-context` flag is the canonical-context guard: when the restore target has a non-empty `required_status_checks.contexts` set that does NOT contain the canonical SCP check (`REQUIRED_CONTEXT`, default `policy-check / scp/policy-check`), restore mode requires this flag, preventing an attacker-modified pre-state JSON from silently replacing the policy gate with attacker-controlled context. (5) Set-equality verify phase replaces the prior comma-substring `grep -F` comparison with a jq `--argjson expected_contexts ... sort == sort` element-wise comparison, so legitimate restores no longer fail spuriously when GitHub returns contexts in a different order than the pre-state JSON. (6) Transform inclusion lists (`ALLOWED_REQUIRED_STATUS_CHECKS_KEYS`, `ALLOWED_REQUIRED_PULL_REQUEST_REVIEW_KEYS`, `ALLOWED_RESTRICTIONS_KEYS`) replace the prior exclusion-list shape in `transform()` — only documented fields pass through to the PUT body; unknown nested fields are dropped rather than leaked. (7) Mutex checks reject incompatible flag combinations (`--restore + --expected-wrapper-sha`, `--restore + --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification`, `--restore + --i-understand-wrapper-inaccessible`, `--expected-wrapper-sha + --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification`, `--expected-wrapper-sha + --i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci`). ADOPT-001 §12.8 Gate 3 is rewritten to use `--expected-wrapper-sha` automated wrapper-pin verification (replacing the manual procedure from extras-1); the Post-break-glass permanence callout is restored. `policies/VERSIONING.md` lists all 4 new posture-degradation flags as breaking-change-protected. | ACCEPTED | The depth-defense surface was operator-decided to carve out of 024B-extras after 16 R-cycles + 38 hours of session wall-time plateaued at ~5 MAJ per round, with most findings being latent depth-defects in the depth-defense surface itself (per `SCOPE-CORRECTION-2-2026-05-11.md` + `feedback_asymptotic_trajectory_split.md`). The core `--restore` + manual-Gate-3 + workflow CI shipped in 024B-extras-1 (D-047); this slice (024B-extras-2) ratifies the depth-defense additions as separable safety properties, each adding a real defensive value: automated Gate 3 SHA-pinning eliminates operator-error on wrapper-pin verification; canonical-context guard prevents pre-state-tampering attacks; set-equality verify removes a brittle string-substring comparison; transform inclusion lists close the lock_branch / required_linear_history / block_creations posture gap (per R30 SAFE-MAJ-001); annotated-tag dereferencing makes Gate 3 work regardless of tag-format choice. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: the helper remains operator-run, the bypass flags are explicitly auditable via stderr CAUTION + log-block CAUTION entries; the 2026-07-21 quarterly review re-evaluates single-operator posture for break-glass procedures. Identified at WP-SCP-024 024B-extras carve point (SCOPE-CORRECTION-2-2026-05-11.md) + D-048 reservation. |\n\n> D-044 limitation note: the CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient. 024B-extras must hardcode `--tooling-slice-id` per workflow file and omit `--allow-not-applicable` from cohort workflows; that workflow-side hardcoding is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001).\n\n=====\n# SCP Federation Primitive — Versioning Contract\n\n**Status:** active as of slice 020H.1 (2026-05-01).\n**Closes:** WP-SCP-020 §4 020H.1 sub-criteria (i) + (ii).\n\n---\n\n## Scope\n\nThis document defines the semver contract that the SCP federation\nprimitive guarantees to adopters who pin a `# tag: v<X>.<Y>.<Z>`\nwrapper at `.github/workflows/policy-check.yml`. It covers three\npublic surfaces:\n\n1. **Reusable workflow inputs** — `workflow_call.inputs` declared on\n   `.github/workflows/policy-check.yml`.\n2. **Schema contracts** — JSON Schema at\n   `schemas/policy-check-summary.schema.json` (the structured\n   summary artefact every adopter consumes for nightly + release-gate\n   reconciliation), `schemas/rule-config.schema.json`, and\n   `schemas/scorecard-emit.schema.json` (the opt-in cross-repo\n   scorecard emit per WP-SCP-023 023B / D-041 — added at v1.2.0;\n   MAJOR-pinned per WP-SCP-023 plan-doc §10 Q1).\n3. **Rule IDs** — the `SCP-R-NNN` identifiers under `policies/`.\n4. **Opt-in workflow inputs** — `scorecard-emit: bool` (added at\n   v1.2.0 per WP-SCP-023 023B; default false). Opt-in inputs follow\n   the same MINOR/MAJOR semver category as core inputs.\n\nIt does NOT cover internal-only surfaces: `lib/policy_check_invocation.sh`,\n`scripts/scp-policy-check.lock`, helper scripts in `scripts/`, the\nRenovate preset internal shape, the OPA Rego `scp_common.rego`\nhelpers, or the SCP-self cross-repo aggregator pipeline\n(`.github/workflows/scorecard-aggregator.yml`,\n`schemas/scorecard-opt-in-registry.schema.json`,\n`schemas/scorecard-index.schema.json`,\n`docs/scorecards/opt-in-registry.yaml`,\n`output/scorecards/index.json` — added in slice 023C / D-042; mirrors\n`.github/workflows/conflict-gate.yml` posture per slice 020N).\nThese are refactored without notice.\n\n---\n\n## Semver guarantee\n\n**`MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`** with strict semantic-versioning meaning:\n\n| Bump | When | Adopter action required |\n|---|---|---|\n| **PATCH** (`v1.0.x`) | Bug fixes, new informational annotations, performance improvements, observability records, internal refactors of helpers/lib. | None — drop in the SHA bump and merge. |\n| **MINOR** (`v1.x.0`) | New `workflow_call` inputs (with safe defaults), new `SCP-R-NNN` rules added with `threshold: warn` baseline, new optional schema fields, new commit-status contexts (informational), new `disabled_rules:` reasons. | Recommended: review the release notes for new rules. None mandatory unless the adopter wants to opt into a new rule's `threshold: deny`. |\n| **MAJOR** (`vX.0.0`) | Breaking changes — see \"What is breaking\" below. | **Mandatory** — read the migration guide in the release notes; an amending decision row in adopter's `DECISIONS.md` (or equivalent) before bumping the SHA pin. |\n\n---\n\n## What counts as a breaking change\n\nAny of the following requires a MAJOR bump and an amending decision row:\n\n### Workflow inputs\n\n- Removing a `workflow_call.inputs.<name>`.\n- Renaming a `workflow_call.inputs.<name>`.\n- Changing the `default:` value of an input in a way that flips an existing adopter's effective behaviour (e.g. `threshold: warn` → `threshold: deny`).\n- Changing the `type:` of an input.\n- Making a previously-optional input required.\n\n### Schema contracts\n\n- Removing a previously-required field from `policy-check-summary.schema.json` or `rule-config.schema.json`.\n- Renaming any field (use add-new-then-deprecate instead).\n- Tightening a string `pattern:` such that previously-valid values are rejected.\n- Tightening a `format:` constraint (e.g. `date` → `date-time`).\n- Adding a new required field (covered by the deprecation ramp — see below).\n- Removing an `enum:` value that was previously emitted.\n\n### Rule IDs\n\n- Removing an `SCP-R-NNN` rule.\n- Re-numbering an `SCP-R-NNN` rule (the ID is the durable contract; the underlying logic is internal).\n- Promoting a rule from `threshold: warn` to `threshold: deny` as the **default** at the SCP source. (Adopters may always override per-rule via `.scp/rule-config.yaml`; the default flip is what's breaking.)\n- Tightening a rule's match scope such that previously-passing manifests now deny.\n\n### Read-back / annotation contracts\n\n- Removing `scp/policy-check-readback` commit-status posting.\n- Removing a `::warning::` or `::error::` annotation surface that adopters' downstream tooling parses (e.g. an annotation parser that grouped on `title=SCP-E001`).\n- Renaming an `SCP-ENNN` error-code identifier.\n\n### Branch-protection helper\n\n- Removing `--repo`, `--branch`, `--plan`, `--no-enforce-admins`, `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate`, `--restore`, `--expected-wrapper-sha`, `--i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement`, `--i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks`, `--i-understand-restore-disables-strict-mode`, `--i-understand-restore-disables-required-signatures`, `--i-understand-restore-replaces-required-check-context`, `--i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes`, `--i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions`, `--i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci`, `--i-understand-no-gate-2-verification`, or `--i-understand-wrapper-inaccessible` flags from `scripts/enable-required-check.sh`.\n- Changing the script's invocation-log markdown block schema.\n\n### Tag protection\n\n- Changing the `v*` or `renovate/v*` Repository Ruleset shape (deletion / non-fast-forward / update behaviour).\n\n---\n\n## What does NOT count as breaking\n\n- Adding new `workflow_call.inputs.<name>` with a safe default.\n- Adding new `SCP-R-NNN` rules at `threshold: warn` baseline.\n- Adding new schema fields (`additionalProperties: false` is preserved — but new fields are added under existing properties, not as required new top-level fields).\n- Adding new `enum:` values that were not previously emitted.\n- Adding new `disabled_rules:` reasons.\n- Adding new informational `::warning::` or `::error::` titles (if existing titles preserved).\n- Internal helper-library refactors.\n- New commit-status contexts (the existing two are stable).\n- New OPA / Conftest / Regal binary version bumps in the lockfile (these are SHA-pinned; adopters get the new pins via Renovate auto-bump).\n\n---\n\n## Deprecation ramp (one-release warning window)\n\n**Closes 020H.1 sub-criterion (ii); enforced by 020H.3 release-gate.**\n\nWhen the SCP source needs to remove or breaking-change one of the\npublic surfaces above, the change MUST proceed via a **one-release\ndeprecation ramp**:\n\n1. **Release N: add the new surface** (alongside the old surface), and\n   in the same release add a `::warning::` annotation to the old\n   surface stating: `<surface> deprecated; use <new-surface>; will be\n   removed in v<X+1>.0.0`. Document in release notes. **Add an entry\n   to `policies/deprecations.yaml`** with `announced_at`,\n   `announced_release`, `target_release`, and `migration_pointer`\n   (per `schemas/deprecations.schema.json`).\n2. **Release N+1 (the next MINOR or MAJOR — whichever lands first):**\n   keep both surfaces live; the warning continues to fire.\n3. **Release N+M (the MAJOR after the next MINOR):** old surface is\n   removed; the MAJOR bump is the breaking-change vehicle. An amending\n   decision row in `docs/DECISIONS.md` records the removal. **Remove\n   the entry from `policies/deprecations.yaml`** as part of the\n   removal commit.\n\nThe minimum gap between deprecation announcement (step 1) and\nremoval (step 3) is **one MINOR release**. Adopters who track every\nMINOR release see at least one full PR cycle's worth of warnings\nbefore the surface vanishes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
